  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nDew points were in the 20s and 30s this past weekend, but will be \nrising through the 40s today through Wednesday. This is as \nsouthwesterly flow Sunday and today promote moistening. Veering \nwinds to easterly occurs Tuesday as a backdoor cold front approaches \nand washes out. As long as east winds continue Tuesday night/Wed AM \nand remain light, fog could occur as marine air is advected \nwestward. A cold front approaches from the northwest Wednesday and \nmay disrupt fog formation if clouds get to those areas early enough. \nThis study of moisture content also begs the question about fire \nweather potential. Well, winds are expected to remain light enough \nto remove fire weather concern and limit that to grass fires at \nbest. Given this mention of dry weather, rain chances will continue \nto be limited to less than wetting through most of Sunday. A cold \nfront approaching late next weekend to early the following week (7-8 \ndays away) would be the next chance to see rain, but it's a small \nchance.\n\nAfter starting this morning with a chance for frost, attention turns \nto tonight to see if we have another chance for frost. This is as \nlong as the growing season remains intact. Given that dew points are \nexpected to warm, low temps will have more of a floor to prevent \ncooling. Additionally, a cold front brings high clouds to the area \nMonday night limiting radiational cooling. Given these factors, have \npassed on issuing anything for frost Monday night. Lows stay more in \nthe 40s for Tuesday night and beyond, meaning our next frost chance \nwon't be for a while now.",
